<td align="left" valign="top">Ac-K382,p-S15,S20-TP53<br>Tumor protein p53, a nuclear protein, plays an essential role in the regulation of cell cycle, specifically in the transition from G0 to G1.  It is found in very low levels in normal cells, however, in a variety of transformed cell lines, it is expressed in high amounts, and believed to contribute to transformation and malignancy.  p53 is a DNA-binding protein containing DNA-binding, oligomerization and transcription activation domains.  It is postulated to bind as a tetramer to a p53-binding site and activate expression of downstream genes that inhibit growth and/or invasion, and thus function as a tumor suppressor.  Mutants of p53 that frequently occur in a number of different human cancers fail to bind the consensus DNA binding site, and hence cause the loss of tumor suppressor activity.  Alterations of the TP53 gene occur not only as somatic mutations in human malignancies, but also as germline mutations in some cancer-prone families with Li-Fraumeni syndrome. [BLAST Evalue to experimental molecule = 4.73e-130]<br>p-S15-TP53<br>SUMO2,3-K386-TP53<br>Me2K-370,382-TP53<br>Tumor protein p53, a nuclear protein, plays an essential role in the regulation of cell cycle, specifically in the transition from G0 to G1.  It is found in very low levels in normal cells, however, in a variety of transformed cell lines, it is expressed in high amounts, and believed to contribute to transformation and malignancy.  p53 is a DNA-binding protein containing DNA-binding, oligomerization and transcription activation domains.  It is postulated to bind as a tetramer to a p53-binding site and activate expression of downstream genes that inhibit growth and/or invasion, and thus function as a tumor suppressor.  Mutants of p53 that frequently occur in a number of different human cancers fail to bind the consensus DNA binding site, and hence cause the loss of tumor suppressor activity.  Alterations of the TP53 gene occur not only as somatic mutations in human malignancies, but also as germline mutations in some cancer-prone families with Li-Fraumeni syndrome. [BLAST Evalue to experimental molecule = 5.41e-116]<br>p-S15,S392-TP53<br>TP53_inactive<br>p-S15,S20-TP53<br>p-S15,S20,S269,T284-TP53<br>p-S15,S33,S46-TP53<br>MeK-370,p-S15,S20-TP53<br>Ac-K120,K382,p-S15,S20-TP53<br>Tumor protein p53 (Li-Fraumeni syndrome); Transformation related protein 53; Cellular tumor antigen p53; Phosphoprotein p53; Antigen NY-CO-13; Tumor suppressor p53. P53 is a transcription factor that is activated in response to DNA damage. Activated p53 stimulates the expression of downstream genes involved in growth arrest, apoptosis, and DNA repair. P53 also activates transcription of the p53 negative regulatory protein MDM2.<br>TP53 gene<br>Tumour Protein p53 (Li-Fraumeni syndrome). Trans-Related Protein p53. Tumour protein p53, a nuclear protein, plays an essential role in the regulation of cell cycle, specifically in the transition from G0 to G1. It is found in very low levels in normal cells, however, in a variety of transformed cell lines, it is expressed in high amounts, and believed to contribute to transformation and malignancy. p53 is a DNA-binding protein containing DNA-binding, oligomerization and transcription activation domains. It is postulated to bind as a tetramer to a p53-binding site and activate expression of downstream genes that inhibit growth and/or invasion, and thus function as a tumour suppressor. Mutants of p53 that frequently occur in a number of different human cancers fail to bind the consensus DNA binding site, and hence cause the loss of tumor suppressor activity. Alterations of the TP53 gene occur not only as somatic mutations in human malignancies, but also as germline mutations in some cancer-prone families with Li-Fraumeni syndrome.<br>P53 messenger RNA (TP53 mRNA)<br>Me1-K372,p-S15,S20-TP53<br>p-S15,S20,S46-TP53<br>p-S15,S20,S392-TP53<br>p-S315-TP53<br>Ac-K120,p-S15,S20-TP53<br>p-T55-TP53<br>Homo sapiens tumor protein p53 (Li-Fraumeni syndrome) (TP53). Refer to BioSeq for sequence information derived from PMID 12808131. Note that the listed BioSeq may not describe the complete TP53 promoter.<br>PolyUb-TP53<br>Unfolded TP53<br>SUMO2 N-glycyl-lys386 TP53<br>Cellular tumor antigen p53 (TP53)<br>p-S37-TP53<br>TP53<br>PolyUb,p-S15,S20-TP53<br>TP53.  Note that due to the absence of an annotated DNA molecule identifier, the listed GI refers to the entire chromosome 17 genomic sequence.  The sequence range of the TP53 promoter was not specified.<br>TP53 promoter<br>Tumor protein p53, a nuclear protein, plays an essential role in the regulation of cell cycle, specifically in the transition from G0 to G1.  It is found in very low levels in normal cells, however, in a variety of transformed cell lines, it is expressed in high amounts, and believed to contribute to transformation and malignancy.  p53 is a DNA-binding protein containing DNA-binding, oligomerization and transcription activation domains.  It is postulated to bind as a tetramer to a p53-binding site and activate expression of downstream genes that inhibit growth and/or invasion, and thus function as a tumor suppressor.  Mutants of p53 that frequently occur in a number of different human cancers fail to bind the consensus DNA binding site, and hence cause the loss of tumor suppressor activity.  Alterations of the TP53 gene occur not only as somatic mutations in human malignancies, but also as germline mutations in some cancer-prone families with Li-Fraumeni syndrome. [BLAST Evalue to experimental molecule = 6.47e-117]<br>Me2-K373-TP53<br>TP53_RNA<br>p-S15,S20,Me-R333,Me2-R335,R337-TP53<br>Me-K382,p-S15,S20-TP53<br>Me2-K370,K382-TP53</td>
